# daemon/sandbox.slice — systemd user slice for the sandbox container.
#
# Why this exists: docker-compose.yml's `memswap_limit` field is silently
# ignored on rootless Docker + cgroup v2 (verified empirically — a 4 GB
# python bytearray inside the container ran to ~5 GB total before getting
# OOM-killed, with peak swap usage 1.95 GB). Docker sets HostConfig.MemorySwap
# in the container spec, but never translates it to the cgroup v2
# `memory.swap.max` interface — the cgroup file stays at "max" (unlimited).
#
# Without enforcement, the container can eat the entire 2 GB host swap pool
# in addition to its 3 GB RAM cap, leaving the host with no swap of its own.
# That defeats the design intent of "swap is the host's emergency reclaim,
# not container expansion space" — see scripts/cloud-init-tasks.sh step 1
# for the broader rationale.
#
# Fix: docker-compose.yml uses `cgroup_parent: sandbox.slice` to place the
# container under this slice. systemd applies MemorySwapMax=0 at the slice
# level on creation; the container's own cgroup inherits memory.swap.max=0
# from its parent. The cgroup OOM killer now fires at exactly mem_limit
# (3 GB RAM, 0 swap) — verified with the same test allocating to 3000 MB
# and killed there cleanly.
#
# This is a USER slice (dev's user@1000.service hierarchy) because rootless
# Docker runs inside the dev user-systemd. Installed by scripts/cloud-init-
# tasks.sh into ~dev/.config/systemd/user/ at provisioning time.

[Unit]
Description=Sandbox slice — denies swap allowance for the agent container
Documentation=https://github.com/merijnvanes/coding-agent-vps

[Slice]
MemorySwapMax=0
